#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:2
msgid "Contributing to PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:4
msgid ""
"If you'd like to contribute to PyBaMM (thanks!), please have a look at the "
"[guidelines below](#workflow)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:6
msgid ""
"If you're already familiar with our workflow, maybe have a quick look at the "
"[pre-commit checks](#pre-commit-checks) directly below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:8
msgid "Pre-commit checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:10
msgid "Before you commit any code, please perform the following checks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:12
msgid "[All tests pass](#testing): `$ nox -s unit`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:13
msgid ""
"[The documentation builds](#building-the-documentation): `$ nox -s docs`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:15
msgid "Installing and using pre-commit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:17
msgid ""
"`PyBaMM` uses a set of `pre-commit` hooks and the `pre-commit` bot to format "
"and prettify the codebase. The hooks can be installed locally using -"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:19
msgid ""
"# Using uv\n"
"uvx pre-commit install\n"
"\n"
"# Or using pip\n"
"pip install pre-commit\n"
"pre-commit install\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:28
msgid ""
"This would run the checks every time a commit is created locally. The checks "
"will only run on the files modified by that commit, but the checks can be "
"triggered for all the files using -"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:30
msgid "pre-commit run --all-files\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:34
msgid ""
"If you would like to skip the failing checks and push the code for further "
"discussion, use the `--no-verify` option with `git commit`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:36
msgid "Workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:38
msgid ""
"We use [GIT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Git) and [GitHub](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/GitHub) to coordinate our work. When making any kind of "
"update, we try to follow the procedure below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:40
msgid "A. Before you begin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:42
msgid ""
"Create an [issue](https://guides.github.com/features/issues/) where new "
"proposals can be discussed before any coding is done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:43
msgid ""
"Create a [branch](https://help.github.com/articles/creating-and-deleting-"
"branches-within-your-repository/) of this repo (ideally on your own [fork]"
"(https://help.github.com/articles/fork-a-repo/)), where all changes will be "
"made"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:44
msgid ""
"Download the source code onto your local system, by [cloning](https://help."
"github.com/articles/cloning-a-repository/) the repository (or your fork of "
"the repository)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:45
msgid ""
"[Install](https://docs.pybamm.org/en/latest/source/user_guide/installation/"
"install-from-source.html) PyBaMM with the developer options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:46
msgid ""
"[Test](#testing) if your installation worked, using pytest: `$ pytest -m "
"unit`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:48
msgid "You now have everything you need to start making changes!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:50
msgid "B. Writing your code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:52
msgid ""
"PyBaMM is developed in [Python](https://www.python.org), and makes heavy use "
"of [NumPy](https://numpy.org/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:53
msgid ""
"Make sure to follow our [coding style guidelines](#coding-style-guidelines)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:54
msgid ""
"Commit your changes to your branch with [useful, descriptive commit messages]"
"(https://cbea.ms/git-commit/): Remember these are publicly visible and "
"should still make sense a few months ahead in time. While developing, you "
"can keep using the GitHub issue you're working on as a place for discussion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:58
msgid ""
"If you want to add a dependency on another library, or re-use code you found "
"somewhere else, have a look at [these guidelines](#dependencies-and-reusing-"
"code)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:60
msgid "C. Merging your changes with PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:62
msgid "[Test your code!](#testing)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:63
msgid ""
"PyBaMM has online documentation at http://docs.pybamm.org/. To make sure any "
"new methods or classes you added show up there, please read the "
"[documentation](#documentation) section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:64
msgid ""
"If you added a major new feature, perhaps it should be showcased in an "
"[example notebook](#example-notebooks)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:65
msgid ""
"When you feel your code is finished, or at least warrants serious "
"discussion, run the [pre-commit checks](#pre-commit-checks) and then create "
"a [pull request](https://help.github.com/articles/about-pull-requests/) (PR) "
"on [PyBaMM's GitHub page](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:66
msgid ""
"Once a PR has been created, it will be reviewed by any member of the "
"community. Changes might be suggested which you can make by simply adding "
"new commits to the branch. When everything's finished, someone with the "
"right GitHub permissions will merge your changes into PyBaMM main repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:68
msgid ""
"Finally, if you really, really, _really_ love developing PyBaMM, have a look "
"at the current [project infrastructure](#infrastructure)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:70
msgid "Coding style guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:72
msgid ""
"PyBaMM follows the [PEP8 recommendations](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/"
"pep-0008/) for coding style. These are very common guidelines, and community "
"tools have been developed to check how well projects implement them. We "
"recommend using pre-commit hooks to check your code before committing it. "
"See [installing and using pre-commit](#installing-and-using-pre-commit) "
"section for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:74
msgid "Ruff"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:76
msgid ""
"We use [ruff](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff) to check our PEP8 "
"adherence. To try this on your system, navigate to the PyBaMM directory in a "
"console and type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:78
msgid ""
"# Using uv\n"
"uvx pre-commit run ruff\n"
"\n"
"# Or using pip\n"
"pip install pre-commit\n"
"pre-commit run ruff\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:87
msgid ""
"ruff is configured inside the file `pre-commit-config.yaml`, allowing us to "
"ignore some errors. If you think this should be added or removed, please "
"submit an [issue](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/issues)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:89
msgid ""
"When you commit your changes they will be checked against ruff automatically "
"(see [Pre-commit checks](#pre-commit-checks))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:91
msgid "Naming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:93
msgid ""
"Naming is hard. In general, we aim for descriptive class, method, and "
"argument names. Avoid abbreviations when possible without making names "
"overly long, so `mean` is better than `mu`, but a class name like `MyClass` "
"is fine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:95
msgid ""
"Class names are CamelCase, and start with an upper case letter, for example "
"`MyOtherClass`. Method and variable names are lower case, and use "
"underscores for word separation, for example `x` or `iteration_count`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:97
msgid "Dependencies and reusing code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:99
msgid ""
"While it's a bad idea for developers to \"reinvent the wheel\", it's "
"important for users to get a _reasonably sized download and an easy "
"install_. In addition, external libraries can sometimes cease to be "
"supported, and when they contain bugs it might take a while before fixes "
"become available as automatic downloads to PyBaMM users. For these reasons, "
"all dependencies in PyBaMM should be thought about carefully, and discussed "
"on GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:102
msgid ""
"Direct inclusion of code from other packages is possible, as long as their "
"license permits it and is compatible with ours, but again should be "
"considered carefully and discussed in the group. Snippets from blogs and "
"stackoverflow can often be included without attribution, but if they solve a "
"particularly nasty problem (or are very hard to read) it's often a good idea "
"to attribute (and document) them, by making a comment with a link in the "
"source code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:109
msgid "Separating dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:111
msgid ""
"On the other hand... We _do_ want to compare several tools, to generate "
"documentation, and to speed up development. For this reason, the dependency "
"structure is split into 4 parts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:113
msgid ""
"Core PyBaMM: A minimal set, including things like NumPy, SciPy, etc. All "
"infrastructure should run against this set of dependencies, as well as any "
"numerical methods we implement ourselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:114
msgid ""
"Extras: Other inference packages and their dependencies. Methods we don't "
"want to implement ourselves, but do want to provide an interface to can have "
"their dependencies added here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:115
msgid ""
"Documentation generating code: Everything you need to generate and work on "
"the docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:116
msgid ""
"Development code: Everything you need to do PyBaMM development (so all of "
"the above packages, plus ruff and other testing tools)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:118
msgid ""
"Only 'core pybamm' is installed by default. The others have to be specified "
"explicitly when running the installation command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:120
msgid "Managing Optional Dependencies and Their Imports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:122
msgid ""
"PyBaMM utilizes optional dependencies to allow users to choose which "
"additional libraries they want to use. Managing these optional dependencies "
"and their imports is essential to provide flexibility to PyBaMM users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:124
msgid ""
"PyBaMM provides a utility function `import_optional_dependency`, to check "
"for the availability of optional dependencies within methods. This function "
"can be used to conditionally import optional dependencies only if they are "
"available. Here's how to use it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:126
msgid ""
"Optional dependencies should never be imported at the module level, but "
"always inside methods. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:128
msgid ""
"def use_pybtex(x, y, z):\n"
"    pybtex = import_optional_dependency(\"pybtex\")\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:134
msgid "While importing a specific module instead of an entire package/library:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:136
msgid ""
"def use_parse_file(x, y, z):\n"
"    parse_file = import_optional_dependency(\"pybtex.database\", "
"\"parse_file\")\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:142
msgid ""
"This allows people to (1) use PyBaMM without importing optional dependencies "
"by default and (2) configure module-dependent functionalities in their "
"scripts, which _must_ be done before e.g. `print_citations` method is first "
"imported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:144
msgid "**Writing Tests for Optional Dependencies**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:146
msgid ""
"Below, we list the currently available test functions to provide an "
"overview. If you find it useful to add new test cases please do so within "
"`tests/unit/test_util.py`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:148
msgid ""
"Currently, there are three functions to test what concerns optional "
"dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:149
msgid "`test_import_optional_dependency`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:150
msgid "`test_pybamm_import`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:151
msgid "`test_optional_dependencies`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:153
msgid ""
"The `test_import_optional_dependency` function extracts the optional "
"dependencies installed in the setup environment, makes them unimportable (by "
"setting them to `None` among the `sys.modules`), and tests that the `pybamm."
"util.import_optional_dependency` function throws a `ModuleNotFoundError` "
"exception when their import is attempted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:155
msgid ""
"The `test_pybamm_import` function extracts the optional dependencies "
"installed in the setup environment and makes them unimportable (by setting "
"them to `None` among the `sys.modules`), unloads `pybamm` and its sub-"
"modules, and finally tests that `pybamm` can be imported successfully. In "
"fact, it is essential that the `pybamm` package is importable with only the "
"mandatory dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:157
msgid ""
"The `test_optional_dependencies` function extracts `pybamm` mandatory "
"distribution packages and verifies that they are not present in the optional "
"distribution packages list in `pyproject.toml`. This test is crucial for "
"ensuring the consistency of the released package information and potential "
"updates to dependencies during development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:159
msgid "Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:161
msgid ""
"All code requires testing. We use the [pytest](https://docs.pytest.org/en/"
"stable/) package for our tests. (These tests typically just check that the "
"code runs without error, and so, are more _debugging_ than _testing_ in a "
"strict sense. Nevertheless, they are very useful to have!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:163
msgid "We use following plugins for various needs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:165
msgid ""
"[nbmake](https://github.com/treebeardtech/nbmake/) : plugins to test the "
"example notebooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:167
msgid ""
"[pytest-xdist](https://pypi.org/project/pytest-xdist/) : plugins to run "
"tests in parallel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:169
msgid "To run unit tests using `nox`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:171
msgid ""
"# Using uv\n"
"uv run nox -s unit\n"
"\n"
"# Or with nox installed directly\n"
"nox -s unit\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:179
msgid "Alternatively, you can run tests directly with `pytest`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:181
msgid "pytest -m unit\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:185
msgid "Writing tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:187
msgid ""
"Every new feature should have its own test. To create ones, have a look at "
"the `test` directory and see if there's a test for a similar method. Copy-"
"pasting this is a good way to start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:189
msgid ""
"Next, add some simple (and speedy!) tests of your main features. If these "
"run without exceptions that's a good start! Next, check the output of your "
"methods using [assert statements](https://docs.pytest.org/en/7.1.x/how-to/"
"assert.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:191
msgid "Running more tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:193
msgid ""
"The tests are divided into `unit` tests, whose aim is to check individual "
"bits of code (e.g. discretising a gradient operator, or solving a simple "
"ODE), and `integration` tests, which check how parts of the program interact "
"as a whole (e.g. solving a full model). If you want to check integration "
"tests as well as unit tests, type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:196
msgid "nox -s tests\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:199
msgid ""
"or, alternatively, you can use posargs to pass the path to the test to "
"`nox`. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:201
msgid ""
"nox -s tests -- tests/unit/test_plotting/test_quick_plot.py::TestQuickPlot::"
"test_simple_ode_model\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:205
msgid ""
"When you commit anything to PyBaMM, these checks will also be run "
"automatically (see [infrastructure](#infrastructure))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:207
msgid "Testing the example notebooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:209
msgid ""
"To test all the example notebooks in the `docs/source/examples/` folder with "
"`pytest` and `nbmake`, type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:211
msgid "nox -s examples\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:215
msgid "Alternatively, you may use `pytest` directly with the `--nbmake` flag:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:217
msgid "pytest --nbmake docs/source/examples/\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:221
msgid ""
"which runs all the notebooks in the `docs/source/examples/notebooks/` folder "
"in parallel by default, using the `pytest-xdist` plugin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:223
msgid ""
"Sometimes, debugging a notebook can be a hassle. To run a single notebook, "
"pass the path to it to `pytest`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:225
msgid "pytest --nbmake docs/source/examples/notebooks/notebook-name.ipynb\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:229
msgid ""
"or, alternatively, you can use posargs to pass the path to the notebook to "
"`nox`. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:231
msgid "nox -s examples -- docs/source/examples/notebooks/notebook-name.ipynb\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:235
msgid ""
"You may also test multiple notebooks this way. Passing the path to a folder "
"will run all the notebooks in that folder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:237
msgid "nox -s examples -- docs/source/examples/notebooks/models/\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:241
msgid ""
"You may also use an appropriate [glob pattern](https://docs.python.org/3/"
"library/glob.html) to run all notebooks matching a particular folder or name "
"pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:243
msgid ""
"To edit the structure and how the Jupyter notebooks get rendered in the "
"Sphinx documentation (using `nbsphinx`), install [Pandoc](https://pandoc.org/"
"installing.html) on your system, either using `conda` (through the `conda-"
"forge` channel)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:245
msgid "conda install -c conda-forge pandoc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:249
msgid ""
"or refer to the [Pandoc installation instructions](https://pandoc.org/"
"installing.html) specific to your platform."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:251
msgid "Testing the example scripts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:253
msgid "To test all the example scripts in the `examples/` folder, type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:255
msgid "nox -s scripts\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:259
msgid "Debugging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:261
msgid ""
"Often, the code you write won't pass the tests straight away, at which stage "
"it will become necessary to debug. The key to successful debugging is to "
"isolate the problem by finding the smallest possible example that causes the "
"bug. In practice, there are a few tricks to help you to do this, which we "
"give below. Once you've isolated the issue, it's a good idea to add a unit "
"test that replicates this issue, so that you can easily check whether it's "
"been fixed, and make sure that it's easily picked up if it crops up again. "
"This also means that, if you can't fix the bug yourself, it will be much "
"easier to ask for help (by opening a [bug-report issue](https://github.com/"
"pybamm-team/PyBaMM/issues/new?template=bug_report.md))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:267
msgid "Run individual test scripts instead of the whole test suite:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:269
msgid "pytest tests/unit/path/to/test\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:273
msgid "You can also run an individual test from a particular script, e.g."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:275
msgid ""
"pytest tests/unit/test_plotting/test_quick_plot.py::TestQuickPlot::"
"test_simple_ode_model\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:279
msgid ""
"If you want to run several, but not all, the tests from a script, you can "
"restrict which tests are run from a particular script by using the skipping "
"decorator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:281
msgid ""
"@pytest.mark.skip(\"\")\n"
"def test_bit_of_code(self):\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:287
msgid "or by just commenting out all the tests you don't want to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:288
msgid ""
"Set break points, either in your IDE or using the Python debugging module. "
"To use the latter, add the following line where you want to set the break "
"point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:290
msgid ""
"import ipdb\n"
"\n"
"ipdb.set_trace()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:296
msgid ""
"This will start the [Python interactive debugger](https://gist.github.com/"
"mono0926/6326015). If you want to be able to use magic commands from "
"`ipython`, such as `%timeit`, then set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:298 ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:351
msgid ""
"from IPython import embed\n"
"\n"
"embed()\n"
"import ipdb\n"
"\n"
"ipdb.set_trace()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:307
msgid ""
"at the break point instead. Figuring out where to start the debugger is the "
"real challenge. Some good ways to set debugging break points are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:310
msgid ""
"Try-except blocks. Suppose the line `do_something_complicated()` is raising "
"a `ValueError`. Then you can put a try-except block around that line as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:312
msgid ""
"try:\n"
"    do_something_complicated()\n"
"except ValueError:\n"
"    import ipdb\n"
"\n"
"    ipdb.set_trace()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:321
msgid ""
"This will start the debugger at the point where the `ValueError` was raised, "
"and allow you to investigate further. Sometimes, it is more informative to "
"put the try-except block further up the call stack than exactly where the "
"error is raised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:322
msgid ""
"Warnings. If functions are raising warnings instead of errors, it can be "
"hard to pinpoint where this is coming from. Here, you can use the `warnings` "
"module to convert warnings to errors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:324
msgid ""
"import warnings\n"
"\n"
"warnings.simplefilter(\"error\")\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:330
msgid ""
"Then you can use a try-except block, as in a., but with, for example, "
"`RuntimeWarning` instead of `ValueError`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:331
msgid ""
"Stepping through the expression tree. Most calls in PyBaMM are operations on "
"[expression trees](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/docs/"
"source/examples/notebooks/expression_tree/expression-tree.ipynb). To view an "
"expression tree in ipython, you can use the `render` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:333
msgid "expression_tree.render()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:337
msgid ""
"You can then step through the expression tree, using the `children` "
"attribute, to pinpoint exactly where a bug is coming from. For example, if "
"`expression_tree.jac(y)` is failing, you can check `expression_tree."
"children[0].jac(y)`, then `expression_tree.children[0].children[0].jac(y)`, "
"etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:338
msgid ""
"To isolate whether a bug is in a model, its Jacobian or its simplified "
"version, you can set the `use_jacobian` and/or `use_simplify` attributes of "
"the model to `False` (they are both `True` by default for most models)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:339
msgid ""
"If a model isn't giving the answer you expect, you can try comparing it to "
"other models. For example, you can investigate parameter limits in which two "
"models should give the same answer by setting some parameters to be small or "
"zero. The `StandardOutputComparison` class can be used to compare some "
"standard outputs from battery models."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:340
msgid ""
"To get more information about what is going on under the hood, and hence "
"understand what is causing the bug, you can set the [logging](https://"
"realpython.com/python-logging/) level to `DEBUG` by adding the following "
"line to your test or script:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:342
msgid "pybamm.set_logging_level(\"DEBUG\")\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:345
msgid ""
"In models that inherit from `pybamm.BaseBatteryModel` (i.e. any battery "
"model), you can use `self.process_symbol` to process a symbol with a fully "
"built model to see what it will look like."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:347
msgid "Profiling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:349
msgid ""
"Sometimes, a bit of code will take much longer than you expect to run. In "
"this case, you can set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:360
msgid ""
"as above, and then use some of the profiling tools. In order of increasing "
"detail:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:362
msgid "Simple timer. In ipython, the command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:364
msgid "%time command_to_time()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:368
msgid ""
"tells you how long the line `command_to_time()` takes. You can use `%timeit` "
"instead to run the command several times and obtain more accurate timings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:369
msgid ""
"Simple profiler. Using `%prun` instead of `%time` will give a brief "
"profiling report 3. Detailed profiler. You can install the detailed profiler "
"`snakeviz` through pip:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:371
msgid "pip install snakeviz\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:375
msgid "and then, in ipython, run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:377
msgid ""
"%load_ext snakeviz\n"
"%snakeviz command_to_time()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:382
msgid ""
"This will open a window in your browser with detailed profiling information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:384
msgid "Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:386
msgid "PyBaMM is documented in several ways."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:388
msgid ""
"First and foremost, every method and every class should have a [docstring]"
"(https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0257/) that describes in plain terms "
"what it does, and what the expected input and output is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:390
msgid ""
"These docstrings can be fairly simple, but can also make use of "
"`reStructuredText`, a markup language designed specifically for writing "
"technical documentation. For example, you can link to other classes and "
"methods by writing ``:class:`pybamm.Model` `` and ``:meth:`run()` ``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:395
msgid ""
"In addition, we write a (very) small bit of documentation in separate "
"reStructuredText files in the `docs` directory. Most of what these files do "
"is simply import docstrings from the source code. But they also do things "
"like add tables and indexes. If you've added a new class to a module, search "
"the `docs` directory for that module's `.rst` file and add your class (in "
"alphabetical order) to its index. If you've added a whole new module, copy-"
"paste another module's file and add a link to your new file in the "
"appropriate `index.rst` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:397
msgid ""
"Using [Sphinx](http://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/stable/) the documentation in "
"`docs` can be converted to HTML, PDF, and other formats. In particular, we "
"use it to generate the documentation on http://docs.pybamm.org/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:399
msgid "Building the documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:401
msgid ""
"To test and debug the documentation, it's best to build it locally. To do "
"this, navigate to your PyBaMM directory in a console, and then type (on GNU/"
"Linux, macOS, and Windows):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:403
msgid "nox -s docs\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:407
msgid ""
"And then visit the webpage served at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`. Each time a "
"change to the documentation source is detected, the HTML is rebuilt and the "
"browser automatically reloaded. In CI, the docs are built and tested using "
"the `docs` session in the `noxfile.py` file with warnings turned into "
"errors, to fail the build. The warnings can be removed or ignored by adding "
"the appropriate warning identifier to the `suppress_warnings` list in `docs/"
"conf.py`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:409
msgid "Example notebooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:411
msgid ""
"Major PyBaMM features are showcased in [Jupyter notebooks](https://jupyter."
"org/) stored in the [docs/source/examples directory](https://github.com/"
"pybamm-team/PyBaMM/tree/main/docs/source/examples). Which features are "
"\"major\" is of course wholly subjective, so please discuss on GitHub first!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:413
msgid ""
"All example notebooks should be listed in [docs/source/examples/index.rst]"
"(https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/docs/source/examples/index."
"rst). Please follow the (naming and writing) style of existing notebooks "
"where possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:415
msgid "All the notebooks are tested daily."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:417
msgid "Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:419
msgid ""
"We aim to recognize all contributions by automatically generating citations "
"to the relevant papers on which different parts of the code are built. These "
"will change depending on what models and solvers you use. Adding the command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:423
msgid "pybamm.print_citations()\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:427
msgid ""
"to the end of a script will print all citations that were used by that "
"script. This will print BibTeX information to the terminal; passing a "
"filename to `print_citations` will print the BibTeX information to the "
"specified file instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:429
msgid ""
"When you contribute code to PyBaMM, you can add your own papers that you "
"would like to be cited if that code is used. First, add the BibTeX for your "
"paper to [CITATIONS.bib](https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/src/"
"pybamm/CITATIONS.bib). Then, add the line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:431
msgid "pybamm.citations.register(\"your_paper_bibtex_identifier\")\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:435
msgid ""
"wherever code is called that uses that citation (for example, in functions "
"or in the `__init__` method of a class such as a model or solver)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:437
msgid "Infrastructure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:439
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:441
msgid ""
"Installation of PyBaMM and its dependencies is handled via [uv](https://docs."
"astral.sh/uv/) or [pip](https://pip.pypa.io/en/stable/). A `uv.lock` "
"lockfile is maintained in version control to ensure reproducible "
"environments. Dependabot keeps the locked dependencies up to date via weekly "
"PRs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:445 ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:467
msgid "Configuration files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:446
msgid ""
"pyproject.toml\n"
"uv.lock\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:451
msgid "Continuous Integration using GitHub Actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:453
msgid ""
"Each change pushed to the PyBaMM GitHub repository will trigger the test and "
"benchmark suites to be run, using [GitHub Actions](https://github.com/"
"features/actions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:455
msgid ""
"Tests are run for different operating systems, and for all Python versions "
"officially supported by PyBaMM. If you opened a Pull Request, feedback is "
"directly available on the corresponding page. If all tests pass, a green "
"tick will be displayed next to the corresponding test run. If one or more "
"test(s) fail, a red cross will be displayed instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:457
msgid ""
"Similarly, the benchmark suite is automatically run for the most recently "
"pushed commit. Benchmark results are compared to the results available for "
"the latest commit on the `main` branch. Should any significant performance "
"regression be found, a red cross will be displayed next to the benchmark run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:459
msgid ""
"In all cases, more details can be obtained by clicking on a specific run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md:461
msgid ""
"Configuration files for various GitHub actions workflow can be found in `."
"github/worklfows`."
msgstr ""

